WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.820
The first film followed nutrients from land to low oxygen.

2
00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:06.960
Where should a limited cleanup budget act?

3
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:13.460
A useful plan cuts the cause first, rebuilds habitat, and works within real limits.

4
00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:21.223
Restoration means returning a damaged ecosystem toward health.

5
00:00:22.543 --> 00:00:27.383
Criteria say what a solution must achieve, such as lowering nitrogen or adding

6
00:00:27.383 --> 00:00:32.243
habitat. Constraints are limits, including money, land, and time.

7
00:00:33.423 --> 00:00:37.203
The budget bar is one constraint, so every choice has a trade-off.

8
00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:44.527
Start upstream. A forest buffer is a strip of trees and shrubs beside a stream.

9
00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:50.527
It slows runoff, traps soil, and can remove nitrate from water underground.

10
00:00:52.087 --> 00:00:55.667
Winter cover crops hold soil and take up leftover nitrogen.

11
00:00:56.947 --> 00:00:59.007
Both act before nutrients reach the Bay.

12
00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:04.207
Wastewater upgrades remove more nitrogen at a treatment plant.

13
00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:06.867
Oyster reefs work differently.

14
00:01:08.227 --> 00:01:11.887
Oysters filter particles, and the reef shelters other animals.

15
00:01:13.347 --> 00:01:16.807
Reefs rebuild habitat, but they do not replace cutting nutrients.

16
00:01:18.227 --> 00:01:21.527
In this plan, underwater grass is a readout, not a tool.

17
00:01:22.947 --> 00:01:26.047
Cleaner water can let it return where conditions suit it.

18
00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:29.630
Place each tool beside the step it can change.

19
00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:34.450
Land and treatment tools can reduce orange nutrient flow.

20
00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:40.710
Reefs add habitat directly, and cleaner water can let underwater grass return.

21
00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:47.350
So a plan cuts nutrients first, then adds habitat as its criteria and budget allow.

22
00:01:48.433 --> 00:01:50.893
No single readout tells the whole story.

23
00:01:52.373 --> 00:01:56.933
Nitrogen load, the total delivered over time, tracks a pressure entering the Bay.

24
00:01:58.473 --> 00:02:01.333
Summer oxygen tracks a condition animals need.

25
00:02:02.573 --> 00:02:04.313
Underwater grass tracks habitat.

26
00:02:05.553 --> 00:02:10.573
A plan may improve one before another, so keep all three gauges beside the same map.

27
00:02:11.583 --> 00:02:14.403
Suppose nutrient flow drops after a new fix.

28
00:02:15.683 --> 00:02:20.203
Which change should appear first: less nutrient pressure, or a fully regrown grass

29
00:02:20.203 --> 00:02:21.203
bed?

30
00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:28.840
Less nutrient pressure can appear first.

31
00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:35.080
Grass recovery often lags: the water must clear, and plants need time to spread

32
00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:41.260
back. Stored nitrogen can also keep leaking from soil for years, so even the

33
00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:42.700
nutrient drop can be slow.

34
00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:47.640
Track each criterion over time instead of judging one sample.

35
00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.880
A strong plan states what evidence would count before work begins.

36
00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.340
Measure the pressure, the condition, and the habitat response.

37
00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.800
Repeat measurements across several years.

38
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.620
Keep the comparison fair, and report when the evidence supports only part of the

39
00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:07.620
plan.

40
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:13.120
Build the plan, measure the results, and compare them with a place that received no

41
00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:18.180
fix. If results are unclear, refine the design and test again.

42
00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:21.400
The causal chain tells you where to act.

43
00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.240
Measured criteria tell you whether the plan works.
